From: fivefold3 <332818675+fivefold3.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: qcom: hand mock_utmi to the core as its reference clock

The qcom,snps-dwc3 binding describes mock_utmi as the clock "needed for
ITP/SOF generation in host mode", i.e. the DWC3 reference clock whose
period the core programs into GUCTL.REFCLKPER and corrects with
GFLADJ.REFCLK_FLADJ. Before the controller node was flattened the child
snps,dwc3 node of IPQ5018/IPQ5332 passed exactly this clock to the core
as "ref"; the flattened core takes no clocks of its own, so nothing
programs the period any more and the controller keeps its reset default
of 52 ns (19.2 MHz).

That happens to be right on the SoCs that clock mock_utmi at 19.2 MHz
(the defaults read back as REFCLKPER=52, REFCLK_FLADJ=200,
240MHZDECR=12/PLS1=1, which is what dwc3_ref_clk_period() computes for
19.2 MHz). On IPQ5332 the GCC only provides 60 MHz, so every microframe
comes out 3.1x too short: SuperSpeed devices still work (no SOF timer
involved) but a high-speed device fails every descriptor read with
-EPROTO. The vendor kernel programs REFCLKPER=16 and REFCLK_FLADJ=0x1459
through its own DT quirks - the same values dwc3_ref_clk_period() derives
from a 60 MHz clock.

Look the clock up by its binding name and store it in the core's ref_clk
before dwc3_core_probe(). The core prepares it (a second reference on a
clock the glue already holds) and uses its rate; the glue keeps owning
it through its clock bulk, so suspend/resume stay balanced.

Tested on GL.iNet GL-BE3600 (IPQ5332): a USB 3.2 flash drive forced to
USB 2 by disabling the SuperSpeed hub port now enumerates and transfers
at high speed; before the change it could not read its descriptor.

---
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom.c
@@ -757,6 +757,24 @@
 
 	qcom->dwc.glue_ops = &dwc3_qcom_glue_ops;
 
+	/*
+	 * mock_utmi is the core's reference clock for ITP/SOF generation (see
+	 * the binding). The flattened core takes no clocks of its own, so hand
+	 * this one over for the GUCTL.REFCLKPER / GFLADJ programming in
+	 * dwc3_ref_clk_period(). On the SoCs that run it at 19.2 MHz this
+	 * reproduces the reset defaults; on IPQ5332 it runs at 60 MHz and
+	 * without it every microframe is 3.1x too short and no high-speed
+	 * device can be enumerated (SuperSpeed does not use the SOF timer).
+	 * The core only prepares the clock and reads its rate; the glue keeps
+	 * owning it through its clock bulk.
+	 */
+	qcom->dwc.ref_clk = devm_clk_get_optional(dev, "mock_utmi");
+	if (IS_ERR(qcom->dwc.ref_clk)) {
+		ret = dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(qcom->dwc.ref_clk),
+				    "failed to get mock_utmi clock\n");
+		goto icc_exit;
+	}
+
 	probe_data.dwc = &qcom->dwc;
 	probe_data.res = &res;
 	probe_data.ignore_clocks_and_resets = true;
